[2026-01-15T22:36:42.003063] Smith-Waterman matrix construction has anti-diagonal wavefront dependencies: each cell H[i][j] depends on H[i-1][j-1], H[i-1][j], H[i][j-1]. Cells on the same anti-diagonal (i+j=constant) are independent and can be computed in parallel. FindHighestScore is embarrassingly parallel. Traceback must stay sequential (path-dependent).

Strategy choice:
1. **CHOSEN: Wavefront/antidiagonal parallelization** - Compute each antidiagonal in parallel, synchronize between diagonals. Deterministic (fixed order), good speedup for large matrices.
2. Row-striping with atomic/lock - Rejected: race conditions at boundaries, non-deterministic without careful synchronization.
3. Blocked tiling - Rejected: complex boundary handling, marginal benefit for typical sequence lengths.
4. Full sequential with parallel FindHighestScore only - Rejected: misses main bottleneck (matrix construction is O(nm), FindHighestScore is also O(nm) but trivially parallel).

Bounded patch: Single file modification (SmithWaterman.cs), ~100 LOC changes within ConstructMatrix and helper methods. TPL Parallel.For with bounded MaxDegreeOfParallelism.

